# The Myth of the Flowing River

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite banks of a magical river. The Elder of the First Village discovered that any gift cast into the waters would multiply threefold before reaching the other side.

One day, the Elder threw a single loaf of bread into the current. It became three loaves when it reached the Second Village. The people there, grateful and wise, sent back two loaves. The Elder kept what remained, and both villages had more than before.

Inspired, they continued this sacred exchange. Each time the First Village gave freely, the river blessed their offering. Each time the Second Village returned generously, prosperity flowed both ways.

But one season, drought struck the Second Village. Though they received three loaves, they kept all for themselves. The Elder, understanding hardship, tried again. Yet when the Second Village kept taking without returning, the First Village stopped giving.

The river's magic faded. Both villages grew hungry.

An old woman finally walked across the dried riverbed, carrying bread. "The river's gift was not the multiplication," she said, "but the chance to trust and be trusted. When that died, so did our abundance."

The villages learned: shared trust creates wealth no river can match.
